|JOIN|

A JOIN combines rows from two or more tables based on a related column.


JOIN syntax:

SELECT columns
FROM table1
JOIN table2
    ON table1.key = table2.key;

Ex:

SELECT *
FROM customers
JOIN orders
    ON customers.customer_id = orders.customer_id;
----------------------------------------------
|INNER JOIN|

#  INNER JOIN returns only records that have a match in both tables.
# only returns matching records.

SELECT
    customers.customer_name,
    orders.order_id,
    orders.amount
FROM customers
INNER JOIN orders
    ON customers.customer_id = orders.customer_id;
------------------------------------------------------
|Why use table aliases?|

#Imagine your query becomes large.

customers.customer_id
orders.customer_id
customers.customer_name
orders.order_id

Ex:

SELECT
    c.customer_name,
    o.order_id,
    o.amount
FROM customers AS c
INNER JOIN orders AS o
    ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;

Now:
c → customers
o → orders

We can omit AS:

FROM customers c
JOIN orders o
------------------------------------------------------------
|LEFT JOIN|

Show all customers, even if they have never placed an order.
keeps all rows from the left table.
The right-side values become NULL when there is no match.

SELECT
    c.customer_name,
    o.order_id,
    o.amount
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
    ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
---------------------------------------------------
|INNER JOIN vs LEFT JOIN|

INNER JOIN---> Give me only matching records.

LEFT JOIN --->Give me everything from the left table, plus matching records from the right.
---------------------------------------------------
Real-world example

Find customers who have never placed an order.

SELECT
    c.customer_id,
    c.customer_name
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
    ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.order_id IS NULL;
---------------------------------------------------------
|RIGHT JOIN|

keeps all rows from the right table.


SELECT
    c.customer_name,
    o.order_id,
    o.amount
FROM customers c
RIGHT JOIN orders o
    ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
-------------------------------------------------------------
|FULL OUTER JOIN|

All matching and non-matching rows from both tables.

SELECT
    c.customer_name,
    o.order_id,
    o.amount
FROM customers c
FULL OUTER JOIN orders o
    ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
----------------------------------------------------

| JOIN              | What it returns           |
| ----------------- | ------------------------- |
| `INNER JOIN`      | Matching rows only        |
| `LEFT JOIN`       | All left + matching right |
| `RIGHT JOIN`      | All right + matching left |
| `FULL OUTER JOIN` | Everything from both      |
